{"name":"Fashion Mauve","hex":"#b5a8a8","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"fashion","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A current (constantly changing) trend, favored for frivolous rather than practical, logical, or intellectual reasons.","Popular trends, especially in clothing; the industry that designs clothing and sometimes other related items."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To make, build or construct, especially in a crude or improvised way.","To make in a standard manner; to work."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/fashion"},{"term":"mauve","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A rich purple synthetic dye, which faded easily, briefly popular c. 1859‒1873 and now called mauveine.","A pale purple or violet colour, like the colour of the dye after it has faded."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having a pale purple colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/mauve"}],"entries":[{"title":"Fashion","blocks":[{"text":"Fashion is a term used interchangeably to describe the creation of clothing, footwear, accessories, cosmetics, and jewellery of different cultural aesthetics and their mix and match into outfits that depict distinctive ways of dressing as signifiers of social status, self-expression, and group belonging. As a multifaceted term, fashion describes an industry, designs, aesthetics, and trends."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a6/Carolina_Herrera_AW14_12.jpg/330px-Carolina_Herrera_AW14_12.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fashion","wikidata":"Q12684"},{"title":"Mauve","blocks":[{"text":"Mauve is a pale purple color named after the mallow flower. The first use of the word mauve as a color was in 1796–1798 according to the Oxford English Dictionary, but its use seems to have been rare before 1859. Another name for the color is mallow, with the first recorded use of mallow as a color name in English in 1611."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/32/Wilde_Malve.JPG/330px-Wilde_Malve.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mauve","wikidata":"Q604079"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/93/Pierre_Balmain_and_Ruth_Ford%2C_photographed_by_Carl_Van_Vechten%2C_November_9%2C_1947.jpg/500px-Pierre_Balmain_and_Ruth_Ford%2C_photographed_by_Carl_Van_Vechten%2C_November_9%2C_1947.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/93/Pierre_Balmain_and_Ruth_Ford%2C_photographed_by_Carl_Van_Vechten%2C_November_9%2C_1947.jpg/1280px-Pierre_Balmain_and_Ruth_Ford%2C_photographed_by_Carl_Van_Vechten%2C_November_9%2C_1947.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/93/Pierre_Balmain_and_Ruth_Ford%2C_photographed_by_Carl_Van_Vechten%2C_November_9%2C_1947.jpg/500px-Pierre_Balmain_and_Ruth_Ford%2C_photographed_by_Carl_Van_Vechten%2C_November_9%2C_1947.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APierre_Balmain_and_Ruth_Ford%2C_photographed_by_Carl_Van_Vechten%2C_November_9%2C_1947.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/78/Siegel_%26_Stockman._The_Brandery_Winter_Edition_2010.jpg/500px-Siegel_%26_Stockman._The_Brandery_Winter_Edition_2010.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/78/Siegel_%26_Stockman._The_Brandery_Winter_Edition_2010.jpg/1280px-Siegel_%26_Stockman._The_Brandery_Winter_Edition_2010.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/78/Siegel_%26